  "source_text": "Chapter Sixty Six.\n\nMonday, 12:17 p.m. CET — twenty four hours remaining — London\n\nThe safe house was above a chip shop in Brixton, south London, and smelled of vinegar and frying oil. Nadia had arrived seven hours earlier on the reserved flight from Zurich, then a courier's car. She had barely noticed the room before laying out the phones.\n\nThe flat belonged to Danny, a Jamaican-British former postal worker who had spent thirty-two years delivering letters for the Royal Mail and had, in that time, developed an encyclopedic knowledge of London's geography, an unshakeable commitment to showing up on time, and an absolute contempt for any institution that failed to do the same. He'd been fired after whistleblowing about postal route changes designed to cut services to low-income neighborhoods. Nadia had covered the story in 2019, when no other journalist would touch it. Danny had never forgotten.\n\n\"Anything you need,\" he'd said when she called. \"The flat's yours. I'll be downstairs running the shop.\"\n\nWhat Nadia needed was a table, six burner phones, a stack of paper, three pencils, and silence. Danny provided all of these, along with unsolicited portions of fish and chips that appeared at the top of the stairs every four hours with the regularity of a man who had spent three decades making deliveries.\n\nNadia sat at the table and ran the switchboard.\n\nThis was the heart of the operation now. Not a server farm. Not an AI agent. Not an encrypted network humming in an Icelandic data center powered by volcanic energy. Just a woman with phones and a list of numbers and the discipline to make each call count.\n\nEach team member reported through a relay known to Nadia. Most messages passed from a short call to a courier and then a dead drop. Emergency lines allowed direct contact when delay was the greater risk. The system kept complete instructions out of any one intercepted call. It could still lose a courier, a phone, a person. Her job was to notice the missing link before the next task depended on it.\n\nShe had nineteen burner phones remaining. At the current rate of consumption, she would run out in approximately twelve hours. She had already dispatched Danny's nephew, a seventeen-year-old named Devon who asked no questions and rode a bicycle with the kind of velocity that suggested a promising career in either cycling or crime, to purchase additional phones from shops across south London.\n\nThe status board was on the wall. Paper, pinned to a corkboard Danny had mounted for her. Each team member's codename, status, and last check-in time.\n\nScaffold (Ines): Active. Berlin. Network online — 3,100 active of 11,000 total; remainder went dark during AI compromise. Ready for data. Last check-in: 2 hours ago.\n\nLocksmith (Andrei): Active. Tallinn. Five exploits verified clean; Barrington package has a conditional route. Drives in courier chain. Last check-in: 2 hours ago.\n\nCourier (Tomás): Active. In transit Lisbon to Austin. Three devices with him; three in secondary courier chains. Estimated arrival: 3 hours. Last check-in: 1 hour ago.\n\nAuditor (Sophie): Active. Geneva. Financial forensics framework 70% complete. Last check-in: 1 hour ago.\n\nTimekeeper (Ólafur): Active. Portable timing system rebuilt in London. Reserved flight to Copenhagen; final simulations will continue there. Last check-in: 90 minutes ago.\n\nAdrian: Active. Amsterdam. Final map corrections in progress. Completion expected by noon. Last check-in: 2 hours ago.\n\nSeven people. European safe houses, one short relocation flight, and one westbound charter. Twenty-four hours remaining. All alive. All working.\n\nNadia studied the board and felt the weight of what it represented. Every name was a life she was responsible for. Every check-in was a confirmation that someone she'd spoken to was still breathing. The gaps between check-ins were holes in the world, minutes and hours during which anything could happen, and she would not know until the next call came.\n\nOr didn't come.",
